# Copy this to cameras.yaml and edit for your cameras:
#     cp cameras.yaml.example cameras.yaml   (or copy on Windows)
# cameras.yaml is gitignored so your real camera names/layout stay private.
#
# Each top-level key MUST match the BlueIris camera name (the &CAM macro)
# exactly, including case and hyphens.
#
#   prompt              the question + the allowed LABELS for this camera.
#                       ALWAYS include a "nothing" label (e.g. NONE) -- the
#                       vision model is the only filter, so most frames are
#                       routine and it must be able to say so.
#   alert_on            labels that should notify. The model's first-line LABEL
#                       is matched (substring, case-insensitive) against this.
#                       Leave the "nothing" label OUT of this list. Every label
#                       here must also appear in the prompt (tests enforce this).
#   notify_cooldown_sec don't re-email for this camera within this many seconds
#                       of the last alert (stops repeat motion from spamming).
#
# The model replies with the LABEL on line 1 and a short description after it;
# the description is what goes in the notification email.
#
# TIP -- log-only labels: any label you put in the prompt but LEAVE OUT of
# alert_on is still classified and written to events.db, it just won't email
# you. This is the recommended way to try a new label: add it, watch it with
#     python review.py --counts
# for a while, then promote the accurate ones into alert_on once you trust them.
#
# GOTCHA -- alert_on matches by SUBSTRING. A label that CONTAINS an alerting
# token will itself alert: with "DOOR_OPEN" in alert_on, a label named
# "DOOR_OPEN_PARTIAL" fires too. Name log-only variants so they don't overlap.
#
# RISK SCORING (optional, shadow mode) -- the `_risk` block below plus each
# camera's `risk` block drive a rolling risk score: every event adds
#     label weight x camera multiplier x night multiplier
# points, and the total decays exponentially when things are quiet. Blips
# fade; patterns escalate. Scores/tiers are logged to events.db (they don't
# change alerting yet). Labels missing from label_weights score 0. Tune
# weights against your own history with:  python replay.py
# Delete the `_risk` block to turn the feature off entirely.

# ==========================================================================
# FOUR PROMPT RULES, EACH LEARNED THE HARD WAY FROM ~32k REAL CLASSIFICATIONS
# ==========================================================================
#
# 1. STATE LOSES TO IDENTITY -- so state the priority explicitly.
#    A condition visible in EVERY frame (a gate open/closed, a garage door
#    up/down) will beat a person or vehicle present in 4% of frames, because
#    "choose the single best label" invites the model to answer the question it
#    can always answer. Adding three gate labels to a driveway camera here took
#    GATE_CLOSED from 68 to 1,576 hits per week and drove UNFAMILIAR_VEHICLE to
#    ZERO in 7,217 frames -- strangers' cars in the driveway were logged as
#    "the gate is closed". Every prompt below that mixes scene state with
#    occupancy opens with a PRIORITY RULE saying which wins.
#
# 2. NEVER ASK A SUBJECTIVE QUESTION. "a vehicle that looks out of place or
#    unexpected" is not a test the model can apply -- it reasoned its way to
#    NONE on parked strangers' cars, describing "no vehicles that stand out as
#    unusual". Identity checks must be objective and checkable from the pixels:
#    is it THAT car (this color, this body type), yes or no.
#
# 2b. ...AND STATE IT POSITIVELY. Fixing rule 2 by leaning hard the other way
#    ("do NOT ask whether it looks ordinary; use UNFAMILIAR even if it looks
#    unremarkable") produced the exact inverse bug within one attempt: the
#    model correctly read "a white pickup truck" and labeled it
#    UNFAMILIAR_VEHICLE anyway, in 6 of 40 frames -- alerting on the household's
#    own truck. Negative definitions ("NOT the white truck") are the phrasing
#    models follow worst. Define the KNOWN case positively, make unfamiliar the
#    residual, and send genuine uncertainty to the QUIET side:
#        is it a white pickup or a black SUV?  yes -> KNOWN, no -> UNFAMILIAR,
#        can't tell -> KNOWN (never alarm because you couldn't see clearly)
#    Corollary: a new label goes into the prompt but NOT into alert_on until
#    ab_prompt.py shows it behaving. Both of these bugs were caught that way,
#    on stored frames, before anyone was woken up.
#
# 2c. PRECEDENCE MUST BE A NUMBERED PROCEDURE, NOT PROSE. "State loses to
#    identity" as a paragraph left the model to invent its own ordering among
#    the occupancy labels, and it ranked a parked car above a human being
#    ("A person is washing a white pickup" -> UNFAMILIAR_VEHICLE). An explicit
#    "answer in this order, take the first that applies, stop there" fixed it.
#    Same lesson as rule 1, one level down.
#
# 3. NO TIME-OF-DAY LABELS. Don't add AT_NIGHT_PERSON / *_NIGHT variants.
#    risk.py already multiplies every event by the night multiplier using its
#    timestamp, so a night label double-counts -- and in 28 days the model never
#    once emitted AT_NIGHT_PERSON on any camera, so the escalation it was
#    supposed to trigger never fired at all. The clock is the code's job; the
#    model only reports what it can see.
#
# 4. A NEW LABEL MUST NOT OVERLAP A SCENE YOU ALREADY SEE OFTEN. Rules 1-3 are
#    about how a label is WORDED. This one is about whether it should exist at
#    all on a given camera, and no amount of rewording substitutes for it.
#    Adding ANIMAL to a front-yard camera took five prompt versions and five
#    A/B runs, and every one failed somewhere different: blind to people on the
#    sidewalk; people placed on the property; both at once, including four
#    frames labeled APPROACHING_HOUSE (alerting) whose own descriptions said
#    "walking on the sidewalk, moving AWAY from the house"; and finally people
#    dropped to NONE. One frame explained all four --
#        NONE  "a person walking a dog on a leash. The person is on the
#               sidewalk, and the dog..."
#    ANIMAL meant "an animal with no person around it", and a dog-walker is a
#    person AND an animal. The two labels competed for the same frame and the
#    model answered neither. That yard has dog-walkers constantly; the BACK
#    patio camera carries ANIMAL without trouble because nobody walks a dog
#    across it. The label was dropped, not reworded.
#    Before adding a label, grep the descriptions in events.db for the scene it
#    covers. If it co-occurs with a label you already have, you are not adding
#    a category -- you are splitting one, and both halves get less reliable.
#    Corollary on scope: that whole exercise chased a COSMETIC defect (a cat
#    logged as PASSING_BY, a log-only label that never emails). Decide what a
#    fix is worth before the first rewrite, not after the fifth.
#
# A fifth, cheaper rule: end every prompt with "Use ONLY the labels listed
# above" -- without it the model occasionally invents one (BIRD_FLYING,
# MOWING_LAWN), and an invented label can never match alert_on. Audit for them
# with `python review.py --unknown`; cam_watcher tags them "unknown-label" in
# events.db as they happen.

# --- Risk scoring (global) ------------------------------------------------
_risk:
  half_life_sec: 600            # a lone blip loses half its weight in 10 min
  window_sec: 7200              # events older than 2 h can't move the score
  repeat_dampening: 0.5         # each older repeat of the same camera+label
                                # counts half again -- frames of one ongoing
                                # condition don't stack like distinct signals
  night:
    start: "22:00"
    end: "06:00"
    multiplier: 3.0             # applied to every label by timestamp -- this is
                                # the ONLY place time of day enters the score
  tiers:                        # lower bound -> tier name
    QUIET: 0                    # log only
    NOTICE: 20                  # email
    ELEVATED: 50                # email, flagged subject (planned)
    URGENT: 80                  # urgent channel (planned)

# --- Pipeline self-watchdog (optional) ------------------------------------
# Run watchdog.py on a schedule (Task Scheduler, every 5 min). Without it,
# nothing tells you the pipeline died -- silence is this system's normal
# output, so a dead Ollama and a quiet afternoon look identical. Delete this
# block to disable. Every value below is optional; defaults live in
# watchdog.py DEFAULTS and are merged under whatever you set here.
_watchdog:
  enabled: true
  stale:                        # nothing arriving at all
    floor_min: 45               # never alarm before 45 min of silence by day
    night_floor_min: 180        # ...or 3 h inside the _risk night window, where
                                # "quiet house" and "dead pipeline" look alike
    multiplier: 2.5             # or 2.5x the p99 gap normal for THIS hour of
                                # day, learned from your own log -- event rates
                                # swing ~40x between 3am and noon, so a single
                                # fixed threshold cannot work
    ceiling_min: 240            # cap: also the longest gap the learner will
                                # absorb, so a past outage can't teach it to
                                # sleep through the next one
    history_days: 14
    min_samples: 30             # below this, fall back to the floor
  errors:                       # arriving but not classifying
    window_min: 30              # THIS is the check that catches a dead model:
    min_events: 5               # events keep flowing and every one fails, so a
    max_fail_ratio: 0.5         # silence check sees a healthy rate and shrugs
  ollama:
    timeout_sec: 5
    check_model: true           # also verify OLLAMA_MODEL is actually loaded --
                                # a renamed model looks just like a dead network
  disk:
    min_free_gb: 5
  notify:
    renotify_min: 180           # an ongoing outage repeats at most every 3 h;
                                # at a 5-minute cadence an untamed watchdog
                                # sends 84 emails per outage and trains you to
                                # ignore it
    recovery: true              # ...and says when it comes back

# --- Example: a front-of-house / entry camera ----------------------------
# Describe the scene concretely (near/far, entry points, which way is "toward
# the house") -- the model classifies far better with a spatial mental map.
# Note the PRIORITY RULE: without it, a catch-all like PASSING_BY quietly
# swallows people who are actually on your property.
front-cam:
  prompt: >
    This is a front-yard / entry camera. The house is on the near side, behind
    the front landscaping. A walkway runs from the driveway to the porch, where
    the front door is (a house entry point). The street is on the far side --
    a person moving toward the street is moving away from the house.

    PRIORITY RULE, read before choosing: if a person is ON THE PROPERTY -- in
    the yard, on the walkway, on the porch, or in the driveway -- they are NOT
    just passing by. Choose PASSING_BY only for someone on the public sidewalk
    or the street, or a vehicle on the street.

    Choose the single best label for the main activity:
    APPROACHING (a person on the walkway, on the porch, or standing at the front
    door, or a person in the yard or driveway moving toward the house. Anyone
    who has left the sidewalk and is headed toward the house belongs here,
    including a visitor who looks perfectly ordinary),
    LOITERING (a person who is on the property but not going anywhere --
    standing or wandering with no apparent task -- NOT someone doing yard work;
    that is YARD_WORK),
    LOOKING_IN (a person with their face close to a window or the front-door
    glass, hands cupped against the glass or shielding their eyes to see inside,
    or a person standing still at close range facing the camera directly),
    YARD_WORK (a person doing routine yard or house maintenance -- watering,
    mowing, gardening, trimming, sweeping, or taking out bins),
    PASSING_BY (a person on the public sidewalk, or a vehicle on the street,
    simply moving past. Someone in the yard, on the walkway, on the porch, or in
    the driveway is NOT passing by),
    DELIVERY (a package delivery in progress -- a UPS, FedEx, or Amazon van or
    truck, or a uniformed courier carrying or dropping off a package),
    MAIL_TRUCK (a USPS mail carrier or mail truck delivering ordinary mail),
    MULTIPLE_PEOPLE (a group of two or more people together),
    NONE (nothing relevant visible -- including an empty yard, weather, moving
    shadows or sunlight, a bird, a cat, or a passing insect).

    Use ONLY the labels listed above. Never invent a new label. Then briefly
    describe what you see, and say where the person is (sidewalk, yard, walkway,
    porch, driveway) whenever there is one.
  # MAIL_TRUCK / LOOKING_IN / MULTIPLE_PEOPLE are logged only (left out of
  # alert_on) until you've confirmed the model uses them well.
  alert_on: ["LOITERING", "APPROACHING", "DELIVERY"]
  notify_cooldown_sec: 300
  risk:
    multiplier: 1.0             # street-facing: noisy, least alarming view
    label_weights:
      LOOKING_IN: 40
      LOITERING: 30
      APPROACHING: 20
      MULTIPLE_PEOPLE: 12
      DELIVERY: 5
      MAIL_TRUCK: 2
      PASSING_BY: 2             # routine, but twenty in ten minutes should register
      YARD_WORK: 2              # give household routine somewhere to land (log-only)

# --- Example: a driveway camera ------------------------------------------
# This one demonstrates all three rules at once, because it is the camera that
# taught them: an always-visible gate, a "does it belong here" question, and a
# night variant. Swap in your own vehicles -- and describe them by COLOR and
# BODY TYPE, which the model can actually check, not by whether they "belong".
driveway-cam:
  prompt: >
    This is a driveway camera looking down the driveway toward the street. A
    gate crosses the driveway and is normally closed.

    ANSWER IN THIS ORDER. Take the FIRST step that applies and stop there:
    1. Is a person ON THE PROPERTY -- in the driveway, in the gateway, or on
       the walkway to the house? Label the person, even if a vehicle is also in
       the frame. A person washing, loading, or standing beside a car is still
       a person. A person out on the PUBLIC sidewalk beyond the gate, or in the
       street, or in a neighbor's yard, is NOT on the property -- ignore them
       and continue to step 2.
    2. Otherwise, is a vehicle in the driveway or moving through the gateway?
       Identify the vehicle.
    3. Otherwise the driveway is empty: report the gate's state. This is the
       normal, expected answer for this camera.
    4. Only if you genuinely cannot make out the driveway or the gate at all:
       NONE.

    IDENTIFYING A VEHICLE. Two vehicles live here: a SILVER sedan and a RED
    pickup truck. Ask one question: is this vehicle a silver sedan, or a red
    pickup? If YES it is KNOWN_VEHICLE -- ours, whatever it is doing: parked,
    moving, doors open, dirty, or covered in soap. If NO -- any other color or
    body type -- it is UNFAMILIAR_VEHICLE. If you cannot tell the color or body
    type, choose KNOWN_VEHICLE; never raise an alarm because you could not see
    clearly.

    Choose the single best label:
    PERSON (a person in the driveway, in the gateway, or on the walkway to the
    house, whatever else is in the frame. NOT someone out on the public
    sidewalk or in the street -- a front-of-house camera covers those),
    MULTIPLE_PEOPLE (two or more people together),
    LOOKING_IN (a person with their face close to a window or vehicle glass,
    hands cupped against it, or standing still at close range facing the camera),
    DELIVERY (a package delivery in progress -- a UPS, FedEx, or Amazon van or
    truck, or a uniformed courier carrying or dropping off a package),
    MAIL_TRUCK (a USPS mail carrier or mail truck delivering ordinary mail),
    KNOWN_VEHICLE (our silver sedan or our red pickup, in the driveway, with no
    person visible. Name its color and body type in your description),
    UNFAMILIAR_VEHICLE (a vehicle in the driveway or gateway that is neither a
    silver sedan nor a red pickup -- for example a black, white, blue, or gray
    car, an SUV, or a van -- with no person visible. Name its color and body
    type in your description),
    GATE_CLOSED (the driveway is empty and the gate is shut, blocking the
    driveway -- the normal state, and the right answer for most frames. At night
    or in infrared, if the gate position is uncertain, choose this),
    GATE_OPEN (the driveway is empty and the gate is clearly open or partly
    open, leaving a gap a vehicle could drive through. Not for a gate merely
    seen at an angle, partially hidden, or in shadow),
    NONE (you cannot make out the driveway or the gate at all -- glare,
    obstruction, or total darkness. A vehicle out on the street, in a neighbor's
    driveway, or parked in the distance beyond the gate is NOT in our driveway:
    if our driveway itself is empty, use a gate label, not this).

    Use ONLY the labels listed above. Never invent a new label. Then briefly
    describe what you see. If a vehicle is present, always give its color and
    body type and say whether it is parked, arriving, or leaving. If a person is
    present, say where they are and what they are doing.
  # KNOWN_VEHICLE is log-only by design -- it IS the known-entity allowlist:
  # your own cars get recorded and never email you. Note there is no
  # VEHICLE_ARRIVING / VEHICLE_LEAVING label: direction from a single still was
  # never once produced in 7,217 frames, so motion goes in the description
  # instead of costing two labels' worth of the model's attention.
  #
  # UNFAMILIAR_VEHICLE goes into alert_on only AFTER ab_prompt.py shows it
  # calling your own cars KNOWN_VEHICLE across a day's frames. A
  # vehicle-identity label is exactly the kind that reads as working until you
  # measure it (rule 2b): the first attempt at this prompt alerted on the
  # household's own truck 6 times in 40 frames. Start it log-only, measure,
  # then promote -- and note that measuring "no false alarms" is not the same
  # as measuring "detects a real stranger", which needs a stranger to show up.
  alert_on: ["UNFAMILIAR_VEHICLE", "PERSON", "DELIVERY"]
  notify_cooldown_sec: 1800

  # --- vehicle_check: the second-call identity check (optional) -------------
  # Absent block = off, which is the right default for a new camera.
  #
  # WHY IT EXISTS. On this project's real driveway camera UNFAMILIAR_VEHICLE
  # fired 0 times in 57,948 events, and six prompt rewrites over three weeks did
  # not change that. The measurement that explains it: the prompt was edited to
  # claim the household cars were "a red sedan and a dark green hatchback", then
  # shown a white pickup -- and the model still answered KNOWN_VEHICLE, in 47 of
  # 48 draws. In a fifteen-label prompt the identity comparison is one clause
  # among many and the model satisfices: KNOWN_VEHICLE becomes its word for "a
  # vehicle is present". Deleting the "if you cannot tell, choose KNOWN_VEHICLE"
  # fallback did not help either -- those frames became NONE.
  #
  # So ask the identity question BY ITSELF, in a second call, on the frames that
  # already have a vehicle label. Measured: 4/4 correct on vehicles that were
  # not ours, 5/5 correct on ones that were, 18/18 draws unanimous.
  #
  # DO NOT "improve" this by asking for colour and body type and comparing them
  # in code. That was built first and measured worse: the household pickup came
  # back "white suv" and, cropped, "silver sedan" -- both 4/4 unanimous -- and
  # produced false alarms on the household's own truck. The same model gets the
  # OURS/NOT-OURS verdict right while describing the vehicle wrongly. Ask for
  # the decision, not for the evidence.
  #
  # COST: one extra call, only on frames whose first answer is in `triggers`.
  # An empty driveway (~85% of frames here) pays nothing.
  vehicle_check:
    enabled: true
    triggers: ["KNOWN_VEHICLE", "UNFAMILIAR_VEHICLE"]
    # Must match how the camera prompt AND the prompt below describe your cars.
    # test_config enforces that all three agree: drift is silent, and its
    # symptom is an email about your own vehicle.
    known: ["silver sedan", "red pickup"]
    known_label: KNOWN_VEHICLE
    unknown_label: UNFAMILIAR_VEHICLE
    # Must ask for OURS / NOT-OURS -- that is all vehicle_id.py understands.
    # The closing default is the 3am rule again: on doubt, say the calm thing.
    prompt: >-
      Two vehicles belong to this household: a SILVER SEDAN and a RED PICKUP
      TRUCK (open cargo bed behind the cab). Look at the vehicle on the driveway
      nearest the camera. Ignore any vehicle out in the street or in a driveway
      across the street. Is it one of those two? Answer exactly OURS or
      NOT-OURS, then in three words say what you see. If you cannot see it well
      enough, answer OURS.

  risk:
    multiplier: 1.2             # on the property, but lots of household traffic
    label_weights:
      LOOKING_IN: 40
      UNFAMILIAR_VEHICLE: 25
      PERSON: 12                # repeats escalate; one sighting doesn't
      MULTIPLE_PEOPLE: 12
      GATE_OPEN: 8
      DELIVERY: 5
      MAIL_TRUCK: 2
      KNOWN_VEHICLE: 0          # your own vehicles -- known entity, never scores

# --- Example: an inside-the-garage camera --------------------------------
garage-cam:
  prompt: >
    This camera is inside a garage. The overhead garage door should normally be
    closed. The household cars are usually parked inside. The overhead door
    slides up to open and down to close.

    PRIORITY RULE, read before choosing: the door is visible in every frame, so
    door state is the fallback, not the answer. If a person is in the garage,
    label PERSON even if the overhead door is also open. Use DOOR_OPEN and
    CLOSED only when nobody is present.

    Choose the single best label:
    PERSON (a person is inside the garage, whatever the door is doing),
    DOOR_OPEN (nobody is present and the overhead garage door is open or partly
    open),
    CLOSED (nobody is present and the overhead door is closed),
    NONE (nothing relevant visible and the door position cannot be made out).

    Use ONLY the labels listed above. Never invent a new label. Then briefly
    describe what you see, including whether the door is open or closed.
  # A "VEHICLE_MISSING" label (door closed + expected car gone) was tried here
  # and retired: 0 uses in 561 frames, and even working it would be noise -- a
  # car out during the day is routine, and one still frame cannot tell "stolen"
  # from "at the store". Counting absent objects is a poor fit for this model.
  # DOOR_OPEN is deliberately NOT here. A door stays open for hours, so an
  # alerting state label re-fires every cooldown for as long as the condition
  # lasts: replayed over 28 days it was 147 of 157 emails from this camera. The
  # decisive part is subtler -- the cooldown is PER CAMERA, so the state label
  # holds it open and suppresses the occupancy label underneath. Removing
  # DOOR_OPEN from alert_on took PERSON alerts from 10 to 20 on the same events.
  # If a state genuinely matters at night, let the risk score's night multiplier
  # escalate it rather than making it alert around the clock.
  alert_on: ["PERSON"]
  notify_cooldown_sec: 1800
  risk:
    multiplier: 1.5             # interior space -- anything odd matters more
    label_weights:
      PERSON: 12
      DOOR_OPEN: 6              # mostly household comings and goings

# --- Example: a back patio / sliding-door camera (with a known pet) -------
# You can give the model a label for a specific known subject (e.g. a pet) and
# leave it out of alert_on so it's logged but never emails. REX below is a
# made-up dog -- describe your own pet's size and color so the model can try to
# distinguish it from a stray ANIMAL. Expect that to be imperfect: on real data
# the same "small light-brown terrier by the door" got the pet label 4 times and
# ANIMAL 3 times. That split is survivable ONLY because the pet label is the
# quiet one -- a mix-up costs a spurious email, never a missed intruder. Put the
# known-entity label in alert_on and you invert that trade.
patio-cam:
  prompt: >
    This camera covers a back patio and a sliding glass door (a house entry
    point) plus part of the back yard.

    PRIORITY RULE, read before choosing: label only what is OUTSIDE. People or
    pets seen through the glass inside the house are not activity -- that is
    NONE. If both a person and an animal are outside, label the person.

    Choose the single best label:
    PERSON_AT_DOOR (a person on the patio or approaching the sliding door),
    MULTIPLE_PEOPLE (a group of two or more people together outside),
    LOITERING (a person who is outside but not coming to or going from the door
    -- standing or wandering in the yard, along the fence or gate, or beside the
    windows, with no apparent task. Someone working in the yard or using the
    patio normally is not loitering),
    LOOKING_IN (a person with their face close to the sliding-door glass or a
    window, hands cupped against the glass or shielding their eyes to see
    inside, or standing still at close range facing the camera directly),
    REX (our dog: a large brown dog that lives here -- use this label for that
    specific dog),
    ANIMAL (any other animal, such as a stray dog, deer, cat, or raccoon. If a
    dog is large and brown, prefer REX),
    NONE (nothing relevant visible -- including an empty patio, weather, moving
    shadows or sunlight, and anyone or anything seen inside through the glass).

    Use ONLY the labels listed above. Never invent a new label. Then briefly
    describe what you see.
  # REX is intentionally omitted from alert_on: logged, never emails.
  alert_on: ["PERSON_AT_DOOR", "LOITERING", "ANIMAL", "LOOKING_IN"]
  notify_cooldown_sec: 300
  risk:
    multiplier: 1.5             # back of house -- higher-signal than the street side
    label_weights:
      LOOKING_IN: 40
      LOITERING: 25
      PERSON_AT_DOOR: 12        # household traffic; repeats escalate
      MULTIPLE_PEOPLE: 12
      ANIMAL: 4
      REX: 0                    # the known pet -- never scores

# --- Example: an indoor utility/laundry camera, leak & smoke watch --------
utility-cam:
  prompt: >
    This camera is in a utility/laundry room with a washer and dryer.
    Choose the single best label:
    WATER_ON_FLOOR (visible water, a puddle, or flooding on the floor),
    APPLIANCE_LEAK (water specifically pooling under or around the washer/dryer),
    SMOKE_OR_HAZE (visible smoke or haze in the air),
    PERSON (a person is in the room),
    NONE (nothing relevant visible).
    Then briefly describe what you see.
  alert_on: ["WATER_ON_FLOOR", "APPLIANCE_LEAK", "SMOKE_OR_HAZE"]
  notify_cooldown_sec: 1800
  risk:
    multiplier: 1.0
    label_weights:              # hazards, not intruders: one frame goes straight high
      SMOKE_OR_HAZE: 80         # URGENT immediately
      WATER_ON_FLOOR: 55        # ELEVATED immediately
      APPLIANCE_LEAK: 50        # ELEVATED immediately
      PERSON: 0                 # household doing laundry
